        "[Not *tidy* data!]{style=\"color:purple;\"}\n",
        "\n",
        "# pandas for Tidy Data\n",
        "\n",
        "## The role of the pandas library\n",
        "\n",
        "</br>\n",
        "\n",
        "::: incremental\n",
        "- **pandas** provides functions to manipulate both the structure and the contents of a dataset.\\\n",
        "- It includes tools for filtering, reshaping, cleaning, and combining data.\\\n",
        "- Together with visualization and machine learning libraries, **pandas** prepares your data for analysis.\n",
        ":::\n",
        "\n",
        "## Common functions for tidy data\n",
        "\n",
        "The **pandas** library provides several functions for reshaping, organizing, and completing datasets.\n",
        "\n",
        "</br>\n",
        "\n",
        "Here, we will discuss some of the most common ones:\n",
        "\n",
        "| Purpose                   | Function 1    | Function 2  |\n",
        "|:--------------------------|:--------------|:------------|\n",
        "| **Pivoting**              | `melt()`      | `pivot()`   |\n",
        "| **Splitting / Combining** | `str.split()` | `str.cat()` |\n",
        "| **Missing values**        | `fillna()`    | `dropna()`  |\n",
        "| **Combining tables**      | `merge()`     | `concat()`  |\n",
        "\n",
        "## Example 2\n",
        "\n",
        "</br>\n",
        "\n",
        "Consider the data in the file \"spotify.xlsx\". This dataset contains the global daily plays of the five most popular songs on the Spotify music streaming service in 2017.\n",
        "\n",
        "</br>\n",
        "\n",
        "Let's read the dataset using the `.read_excel()` function from the **pandas** library."
      ],
      "id": "027cb4be"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "spotify_data = pd.read_excel(\"spotify.xlsx\")"
      ],
      "id": "4d04a373",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "Let's preview the dataset."
      ],
      "id": "01b1cb13"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "spotify_data.head(3)"
      ],
      "id": "ee70a720",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## `.melt()`\n",
        "\n",
        "A common problem is a dataset where some of the column names are not names of variables, but *values* of a variable.\n",
        "\n",
        "The `.melt()` function transforms columns into rows (converts data from **wide** to **long** format). Let's apply it to `spotify_data`."
      ],
      "id": "4d15da4b"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "spotify_long = (spotify_data\n",
        "                .melt(id_vars=[\"Date\"],\n",
        "                value_vars=[\"Shape of You\", \"Despacito\", \n",
        "                \"Something Just Like This\", \"HUMBLE.\", \"Unforgettable\"],\n",
        "                var_name=\"Song\",\n",
        "                value_name=\"Plays\")\n",
        "                )"
      ],
      "id": "91ea19c4",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Remark on column names\n",
        "\n",
        "</br></br>\n",
        "\n",
        "Python allows column names to contain spaces and special characters because they are stored as **strings**.\n",
        "\n",
        "For example, columns such as `\"Shape of You\"` or `\"Something Just Like This\"` can be referenced directly inside quotation marks.\n",
        "\n",
        "> When using **pandas**, column names are almost always written as strings enclosed in quotation marks.\n",
        "\n",
        "## The long version of the data\n",
        "\n",
        "- A `Song` column with the names of the songs.\n",
        "- A `Plays` column with the number of plays for each song and date."
      ],
      "id": "40431b04"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "spotify_long.head(4)"
      ],
      "id": "7a95a985",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## `.pivot()`\n",
        "\n",
        "</br></br>\n",
        "\n",
        "The `.pivot()` function is the opposite of `melt()`. We use it when an observation is scattered across multiple rows and we want to convert the dataset back to a *wide* format.\n",
        "\n",
        "</br>"
      ],
      "id": "aaa7560d"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "spotify_wide = (spotify_long\n",
        "                .pivot(index=\"Date\", columns=\"Song\", values=\"Plays\")\n",
        "                .reset_index()\n",
        "                )"
      ],
      "id": "1847ad17",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## The result"
      ],
      "id": "9578b0e6"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "spotify_wide.head()"
      ],
      "id": "6ce253bc",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Example 3\n",
        "\n",
        "</br>\n",
        "\n",
        "Consider an industrial engineer who receives a messy Excel file from a manufacturing client. The data file is called \"industrial_dataset.xlsx\", which file includes data about machine maintenance logs, production output, and operator comments."
      ],
      "id": "ab1f8c20"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "industrial_data = pd.read_excel(\"industrial_dataset.xlsx\")"
      ],
      "id": "639b9ac3",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "</br>\n",
        "\n",
        "We use this dataset to illustrate the functions `.str.split()` and `.str.cat()`.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Let's preview the data."
      ],
      "id": "77a7212f"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "industrial_data.head()"
      ],
      "id": "76db32e4",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## `.str.split()`\n",
        "\n",
        "The `.str.split()` function separates the contents of one column into multiple columns by splitting the text wherever a separator appears. Consider the `Comment` column."
      ],
      "id": "c19fb779"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "industrial_data['Comment']"
      ],
      "id": "b8595a35",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "</br>\n",
        "\n",
        "The column has some values such as \"Requires part: valve\" and \"Delay: maintenance\\n\" that we may want to split into columns."
      ],
      "id": "179617de"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: false\n",
        "#| output: true\n",
        "\n",
        "industrial_data['Comment']"
      ],
      "id": "72ad7b28",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "We can split the values in the column according to \":\".\n",
        "\n",
        "</br>\n",
        "\n",
        "That is, everything before the colon will be in a column. Everything after the colon will be in another column. To achieve this, we use the function `.str.split()`.\n",
        "\n",
        "</br>\n",
        "\n",
        "One input of the function is the symbol or character for which we cant to make a split. The other input, `expand = True` tells Python that we want to create new columns."
      ],
      "id": "66bdac21"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "#| output: false\n",
        "\n",
        "industrial_data['Comment'].str.split(':', expand = True)"
      ],
      "id": "1e561f5d",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "</br></br>\n",
        "\n",
        "The result is two columns."
      ],
      "id": "edfb6224"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "#| output: true\n",
        "\n",
        "split_column = industrial_data['Comment'].str.split(':', expand = True)\n",
        "split_column.head()"
      ],
      "id": "be0725ab",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "We can assign them to new columns in the dataset using the following code."
      ],
      "id": "cab8696e"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "#| output: true\n",
        "\n",
        "augmented_data = (industrial_data\n",
        "                  .assign(First_comment = split_column.filter([0]),\n",
        "                  Second_comment = split_column.filter([1]))\n",
        "                  )\n",
        "augmented_data.head(4)"
      ],
      "id": "36f13619",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## `.str.cat()`\n",
        "\n",
        "</br></br>\n",
        "\n",
        "The `.str.cat()` function performs the opposite operation. It combines multiple text columns into a single column."
      ],
      "id": "941b9fbd"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "combined_column = (augmented_data[\"First_comment\"]\n",
        "                  .str.cat(augmented_data[\"Second_comment\"], sep=\": \")\n",
        "                  )"
      ],
      "id": "16e448d8",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "The first object is the column that starts the concatenation, and the remaining columns are added using `str.cat()`.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Remove columns with `.drop()`\n",
        "\n",
        "</br></br>\n",
        "\n",
        "Next, we add the new column to the original dataset and remove the other columns `First_comment` and `Second_comment` using the function `.drop()` from **pandas**."
      ],
      "id": "1356aa88"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "combined_data = (augmented_data\n",
        "                .assign(Comment = combined_column)\n",
        "                .drop([\"First_comment\", \"Second_comment\"], axis = 1)\n",
        "                )"
      ],
      "id": "2a4ea97f",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "</br></br>\n",
        "\n",
        "The result is in the `combined_data` dataframe."
      ],
      "id": "6ae6705c"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "combined_data[\"Comment\"]"
      ],
      "id": "a788bd86",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "# Basic data wrangling\n",
        "\n",
        "## Data wrangling\n",
        "\n",
        "</br>\n",
        "\n",
        "- Data wrangling is the process of transforming raw data into a clean and structured format.\n",
        "\n",
        "- It involves merging, reshaping, filtering, and organizing data for analysis.\n",
        "\n",
        "- Here, we illustrate some special functions of the **pandas** for cleaning common issues with a dataset.\n",
        "\n",
        "## Remove duplicate rows\n",
        "\n",
        "</br></br>\n",
        "\n",
        "Duplicate or identical rows are rows that have the same entries in every column in the dataset.\n",
        "\n",
        "If only one row is needed for the analysis, we can remove the duplicates using the `.drop_duplicates()` function."
      ],
      "id": "6415ebf9"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "industrial_data_single = (industrial_data\n",
        "                     .drop_duplicates()\n",
        "                      )"
      ],
      "id": "97ed46f3",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## \n",
        "\n",
        "The `industrial_data_single` does not have duplicate rows."
      ],
      "id": "fb9d7369"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "#| echo: true\n",
        "\n",
        "industrial_data_single.head()"
      ],
      "id": "906dca2b",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "## Fill blank cells\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        ":::::: columns\n",
        "::: {.column width=\"70%\"}\n",
        "Sometimes there are columns with missing values. [In Python, missing values are denoted by `NaN` (*Not a Number*).]{style=\"color:purple;\"} \n",
        "\n",
